a fossil skull unearthed in 1996 has  puzzled researchers for decades  they refer to the animal it came from as  strange beast  in the desert of shenzhen china strange  beast is just one of a myriad of fossils  that have been uncovered  painting a picture of the diverse group  of animals that thrived over 14 million  years ago  now a study in science reconstructs what  this strange beast looked like  and how it fit in with neighboring  herbivores  first the researchers started by taking  ct scans of the specimen skull  they found that the outer wall of its  inner ear was very similar to giraffoids  a family of animals with only two living  species  back in the miocene many other kinds of  giraffoids prospered sporting a variety  of bony structures affixed atop their  heads  but the strange beast's headgear stood  out  no antlers here instead it dawned a  thick flat layer of bone  up close the bone was rough suggesting a  tough outer layer coated this bony top  likely made of keratin  the researchers named this animal disco  carrick's cheshire disco carricks  meaning round-plated horn and she  a reference to a single horned beast  from chinese legends  moving down from the skull to the neck  more unusual features appeared  its vertebrae were extremely thick  containing large areas where the joints  touch each other between vertebrae  to see if this sturdily built neck and  durable headgear could have been useful  for combat the researchers analyzed  churches vertebrae to see how much  stress it could withstand  after running simulations of head  butting with and without this extra  overlap they found that the thicker  vertebrae better absorbed the blow of  intense combat and prevented the head  from rotating too far simulations also  revealed that the disc shaped headgear  better protected the brain than the  horns of sheep and musk oxen  next the researchers analyzed isotopes  and checha's tooth enamel to understand  where they lived and foraged  plants animals eat contain different  amounts of carbon 13  while oxygen 18 varies depending on an  animal's source of water  the high average value of carbon 13  suggests that cherche foraged on open  land unlike other herbivores in the  community which stuck to forests  the large range of oxygen 18 values  could mean that she drank water from  multiple sources  or changed where they live depending on  the season  together these isotope findings suggest  that shercha had a distinct range from  other herbivores in the community  giraffoids particularly today's  extremely long neck giraffes have often  been used as a lesson by those  interested in how animals evolve traits  darwin proposed that their elongated  necks evolved from competition with  other browsers  others think that the long necks formed  from necking among males struggling for  dominance  both environment and combat could have  played a role in how the strange beast  got its hat  shercha fed from a different part of the  ecosystem than other herbivores  comparable to the unique area giraffes  browse for food  competition for resources among checho's  males might have led to combat and the  evolution of strongly reinforced heads  and burly necks  who knows what type of wacky headgear  researchers may unearth in the future
 

